at on  in total you know there was this  neoliberal idea  that in the aggregate the u.s would do  better  the economy would in total be better  there would be more total wealth and  lower  overall prices with this change of  moving manufacturing out and creating  service and  information jobs that happened but it  was overwhelmingly concentrated  in certain parts of the country and not  in others but  politics aside it was also  overwhelmingly concentrated for the one  percent  as we see stagnant wages for large  swaths of americans  and huge income and wealth gains for  the top one percent and even groups  smaller than the top one percent so  for some people they  fell for trump's rhetoric around trade  and the middle class  it was bs populist rhetoric it went  nowhere  but there was a group already  predisposed to favor republicans  in many parts of the country rural older  white  who fell for it and uh it has not gone  particularly well for them now  separately if you look at evangelical  and religious voters  they also are predisposed to vote on  abortion lines  trump claimed to be anti-abortion in  2016. he still claims to hillary clinton  was  in favor of a woman's right to choose so  for some folks it's not actually about  economics  it's as simple as as abortion which  continues to be a  not only a voting issue but the voting  issue for many americans so  there's whenever we want to look into  these questions this is part of what i  spoke about earlier this week  in our interview about the us possibly  as  several countries 331 million people  most countries don't have this many  people and  you have radically different uh  not just ideologies but concerns and  day-to-day life  the day-to-day life of someone who lives  in boston and works at a tech firm  has almost nothing to do with the life  of someone who is a  you know rural ohio factory worker  we speak the same language we're  americans etcetera but  these are very lit different day-to-day  experience 